<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Palace State of Zakros</h1><p class="st-meta">1900 BCE – 1450 BCE (approx.) · capital Zakros</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The easternmost Minoan palace on Crete, a harbour centre for trade with Egypt and the Levant, found unplundered with its treasures in place.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Zakros lies in a sheltered bay at the eastern tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, a natural harbour facing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>. A settlement existed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-minoan-period">Early Minoan period</a>, and a palace was built c. 1900 BCE. The surviving palace, smaller than <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/palace-state-of-knossos">Knossos</a> or <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/palace-state-of-phaistos">Phaistos</a>, dates mostly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neopalatial-period">Neopalatial period</a> after c. 1700 BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The palace followed the standard Minoan plan with a central court, storerooms, workshops, a lustral basin and a cistern room. It likely controlled trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and elephant tusks, some of which were found on site. Its treasury held stone ritual vessels, including a rock-crystal rhyton, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-a">Linear A</a> tablets show local record-keeping.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Zakros was destroyed by fire c. 1450 BCE and abandoned so suddenly that it was never looted. It was not reoccupied as a palace. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolaos-platon">Nikolaos Platon</a> excavated it from 1961, recovering one of the richest collections of Minoan objects found in place.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/palace-state-of-zakros.jpg" alt="Palace State of Zakros" width="320" height="193"><figcaption>Image: User:Bibi Saint-Pol · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_Minoan_Crete-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1900 BCE – 1450 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zakros</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Minoan religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Minoan (undeciphered)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Greece</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kato Zakros</dd></dl></aside></div>

